ep answer opens with the deal at a glance, so the state of the deal is the first thing you read. The figures are sample values."],[655,"A card's explanation can be a short list. Here it spells out which signals count as fresh budget before the agent wakes up."],[19,"A category button narrows the grid to one team's agents. Pre-built and Custom badges mark where each agent came from, and both kinds open the same detail page."],[2196,"A clip opens with everything a rep needs to learn from it: the video, who spoke and how much, and a transcript you can search, filter by speaker or download."],[3496,"A common fix is to make key deal properties required before a deal can change stage. Reps then paste a line or two from memory just to move the deal on. The board looks complete, but the values are often guesses, and she likely still rechecks the deals that matter most."],[1911,"A common fix is to open the CRM record, skim old call notes, search the inbox and message the AE for anything missed. That likely takes half an hour per call. Even then, a hint of expansion buried in one of those calls may never reach the CSM."],[234,"A common habit is to paste lines from the latest deck into each request. Some teams send a link and hope it gets used. That probably works some of the time. Old versions tend to linger in shared folders, though, and nobody can tell which copy an answer came from."],[1395,"A company can keep several boards, such as **Sales Forecast**, **Renewals Forecast** and a regional new business board."],[4267,"A conversation library with search, and meeting notes in the CRM"],[4640,"A CRM layer is for revenue leaders who want AI working on their pipeline without a second place to keep it. The CRM stays the record, and Oliv AI fills it from the conversations that never got logged."],[4694,"A CRO keeps one system of record and still gets AI on every deal. The forecast she presents reads from the same CRM her team updates, and the daily guide for sales managers shows 