onversation"],[4647,"Picture a CRO who spent two years folding three regional CRMs into one Salesforce org. Her board pack, the comp plan and every forecast now read from that org. When a vendor pitches an AI tool, her first question is where the data will live."],[1910,"Picture a customer success manager who looks after a book of mid-market accounts. One customer has a renewal call at 4pm, and the CSM last spoke to them six weeks ago. Since then the account has had a support escalation, two calls with an account executive and a run of emails."],[5621,"Picture a first-line sales manager with six reps and a standing Thursday review. It runs ninety minutes, about fifteen per rep. The goal is to go deal by deal and leave with a clear next step on every opportunity that's slipping."],[1763,"Picture a head of revenue operations leaving a meeting with a new rule. From Monday, an SDR passes a meeting to an AE only when the account fits the target segment and discovery notes are complete. Twelve SDRs and twenty AEs need to follow it."],[524,"Picture a revenue operations lead getting ready to switch on an agent for a sales team of 40. The agent can look up Salesforce opportunities, log calls and change fields such as stage and close date. Lookups worry nobody. A wrong stage change, though, lands straight in Monday's forecast call."],[4283,"Picture a revenue operations lead planning a rollout for a 34-person revenue org. Sales development has five SDRs, and support has two agents who answer customer calls. Sales has nine AEs, two sales engineers and a partnerships manager. Post-sale has five CSMs and two onboarding specialists. Around them sit a CRO, two product managers, two RevOps analysts, two engineers and a product marketer who sits in on customer calls."],[664,"Picture a revenue operations manager whose team has just redrawn the opportunity stages. The new rule says every deal that reaches Proposal needs a wider buying committee before pricing goes out. Around sixty dea