d who hasn't, and puts each pre-drafted number next to the deals behind it and last week's figure."],[1400,"Suggested category moves appear in the drawer's deal tabs, grouped by direction, such as **Move down to Most Likely**. Each group shows a deal count and a total, and each deal carries a red flag with its risk reason. One click on the target button moves the deal, and **X** dismisses the suggestion. That feedback trains the agent."],[5600,"Suggested moves go both ways, down for stalled deals and up for ready ones, and each carries the risk reason to raise with the rep."],[3768,"Summary of tomorrow's meetings"],[2189,"Switch to AI Library to see the libraries the agent fills for you, separate from the ones you curate by hand under My Library."],[4989,"Switching the role to Manager adds the forecast and process agents to the feed, which is the team-level view this section describes."],[2447,"Tabs and views"],[1142,"Take a RevOps admin whose opportunity record carries a dozen custom fields beyond the standard set. There is a deck status, a use case, a competitor field and an integration type. Each one feeds a report the CRO opens every Monday, and two of them decide how deals are routed to solutions engineers."],[2512,"Take an account executive with a pricing-stage meeting on Thursday. The buyer's finance lead joined a call about four weeks ago and raised concerns about budget timing. The AE wants to hear exactly how that was put before walking back in."],[5298,"Take an SDR who has booked a first discovery call with a finance VP at a target account. The call is at 2pm, with three other calls before it. The goal is to qualify the account and hand a live opportunity to an account executive."],[656,"Take the Multithreader agent as an example. It has one trigger: it fires on its own when a deal's stage is set to Proposal in the connected CRM. It can also be started by hand with **Run Agent**."],[5507,"Taking notes during sales calls: what to capture as the call happens."],[5350,"Taking notes during sales calls: what to capture once the meeting starts."],[4596,"Talk about the deal with your team"],[1764,"Teams often handle this with a slide, a Slack post and a few CRM field changes, then a short training session. That work tends to stretch over weeks. Until every tool and scorecard catches up, SDRs and AEs may each be running a slightly different version of the rule."],[1126,"Teams on the weekly cadence get a middle path. Reps receive pending CRM suggestions on Friday morning, and unreviewed ones are accepted automatically on Sunday night. The same approval idea applies to other agents, as described in agent tools and run modes."],[965,"Teams on the weekly cadence get pending suggestions on Friday morning, and anything left unreviewed is accepted automatically on Sunday night. RevOps then receives a summary that includes how often each rep accepted the suggestions. How that digest stacks up is covered in How the CRM Manager agent compares, below."],[2091,"Teams that are moving recordings across from another tool can read the guide to migrating from Gong."],[1439,"Teams typically move through all three operating modes (Beta) within 6 to 18 months. Managers stay in the loop in every mode."],[2570,"Ten minutes before the meeting. It shows the meeting details and research on the account and contact."],[4417,"That attribution is what lets a meeting summary or a proposed field update name the buyer behind a statement. For how transcription tools differ on accuracy, Oliv AI's guide to meeting transcription software compares the field."],[2786,"That context comes from the same record the rest of Oliv AI uses. Calls, emails, CRM data, Slack and meetings form one account context that Engage and every agent can read. So a rank in Engage can reflect what a buyer said on a call last week, not only what sits in a sequence tool."],[3416,"That depends on the run mode you set for each Gmail tool. Drafted emails wait for a person, and Comms Manager sends nothing itself."],[1828,"That's expected. Oliver starts from your existing handbook, playbooks and methodology decks, even when they are messy or incomplete."],[255,"That's nine extensions in all, as the upload area lists them."],[3957,"The **Action Items** tab is where the team works on what was agreed. It shows the **Next meeting**, **Next action** and **Next follow-up**, then a task table with an **Assignee** and **Due Date** for each item, and **Add task** for anything missed."],[1608,"The **Action Items** tab turns commitments into work. **Next meeting**, **Next action** and **Next follow-up** cards sit above a **Tasks** table with **Assignee** and **Due Date**. Each task links to the moment it was agreed, and **Add task** adds your own."],[5418,"The **Action Items** tab turns the call's commitments into tasks. Three cards at the top show the **Next meeting**, the **Next action** and the **Next follow-up**, each with a status such as **Accepted** or **Overdue**. Below them, a **Tasks** table lists each task with an **Assignee**, a **Due Date** and a timestamp back to the moment it was agreed."],[2915,"The **Apps** menu in the main navigation opens Engage, Forecast and Enable. Converse has no entry of its own there: its meetings and calls sit under **Activities**. The Forecast app page on oliv.ai shows its opportunity views, and sales methodology tracking covers the frameworks it tracks."],[2389,"The **Ask** button sits at the right of the meeting drawer. You can also ask Oliv AI's Olivia agent about a call in plain language. Ask her to draft the follow-up from your call this afternoon, and she pulls from the meeting transcript and drafts it."],[2378,"The **Attendees** tab sorts the people on the call into **Buying Committee** and **Other Contacts**, with a search box. Each person gets a card with their sentiment, such as **Ally** or **Neutral**, whether they are a **Decision Maker**, and an access level such as **In-Depth Access** or **Limited Access**."],[2375,"The **Coaching** tab grades the call and scores the rep's skills out of 5. Skill groups such as **Solution Presentation**, **Value Storytelling**, **Prospect Engagement**, **Objection Handling** and **Driving Action** each break into sub-skills. A **Summary** card names one **Improvement Required** and one **Strength**."],[2381,"The **CRM Updates** tab is a timeline of the changes made after the meeting. A green check marks a completed change, such as an account summary refresh, a scorecard update or a new contact. A grey clock marks a **Pending Update** that waits for you, such as a stage move."],[164,"The **Goal** on the **Overview** tab is a short statement kept separately from the instructions. The **Instructions** tab is the full account, in three sections."],[1787,"The **History** tab lists every change to the playbook, by your company, by Oliver and by Oliv GTM Engineers. Filters narrow it to **All**, **By Oliver**, **By Your Company** or **By Oliv GTM Engineer**. Each card shows the date, the author, a status, a summary and the number of files changed."],[2804,"The **Inbox** collects prospect replies from email, LinkedIn and WhatsApp in one list. Each row shows a channel badge, the sender, a one-line preview and when it arrived."],[256,"The **Instructions** tab says what an agent is for and the steps it takes. Knowledge is the background it brings to those steps. Read the two together when you check an agent before a rollout."],[222,"The **Knowledge** tab is one column with two sections."],[2671,"The **Meetings** page in Oliv AI, under **Activities**."],[3979,"The **Meetings** page in Oliv AI, under **Activities**."],[4124,"The **Meetings** page in Oliv AI, under **Activities**."],[1422,"The **New Cadence** wizard has three steps: **Overview**, **Boards & Measurements** and **People**. On **Overview**, give the cadence a **Title** and a **Description**, then choose its **Period**: **Weekly**, **Monthly** or **Quarterly**. With a monthly period, set the **Rep Submission Day** and the **Manager Submission Day** by week and day."],[162,"The **Output** section is hidden when an agent has no output items stored."],[131,"The **Overview** tab gives a short **Goal** statement and lists the agent's tools as pills. The **Instructions** tab is the longer account of the same agent."],[259,"The **Overview** tab lists an agent's knowledge as pills under a **Knowledge** heading. It's the quickest place to see what background an agent has, from the agents in the marketplace to the ones your team runs."],[553,"The **Overview** tab lists an agent's tools as pills. The **Tools** tab is where you set each tool's run mode."],[696,"The **Overview** tab lists the agent's triggers as pills under a **Triggers** heading. Pressing a pill opens the **Triggers** tab, so it's a quick way to check what starts an agent you're about to rely on. Agents with no **Triggers** tab still list their triggers here, each pill with its own icon."],[380,"The **Overview** tab's **Runs** section also carries two summary cards. **Last run** shows a date, with a line beneath describing how it started. The second card shows the week's run count, split into automated and manual."],[2373,"The **Recording** tab holds the player, with **Add Comment**, **Clip** and **Share** beneath it. A **Timeline** groups speakers by company and shows each person's **Talk time** and **Engagement**. On the right, **Chapters**, **Transcripts** and **Comments** let you jump to a section, read along or see your team's notes."],[643,"The **Triggers** tab answers one question: what makes this agent start work by itself? Its own description puts it plainly: \"Triggers allow your agent to work on auto-pilot.\" The tab has two sections, stacked in one column."],[359,"The **Type** column says whether a run was **Auto-triggered** or **Manually run**. Some agents label the same thing **Auto Trigger** or **Manual**. The **Status** column shows how the run ended:"],[4897,"The account executive is the champion, because the time savings show up before and after every call. VPs of Sales and sales managers usually make the purchase. It matters most for reps with a full calendar of meetings on open deals."],[4986,"The account executive's view: the agents that prep meetings, find accounts and keep the CRM current all report into one feed."],[5311,"The actions agents take in your calendar, mailbox and CRM are listed on each agent's **Tools** tab. Agent tools and run modes explains how to choose which of those actions wait for your approval."],[1628,"The Active framework decides which criteria every call is checked against. Set it here before the first meetings, so gap flags match the way your team qualifies."],[1679,"The AE deal workflow: how a rep's week runs across agents."],[12,"The agent builder is where you see what an Oliv AI agent is and how it works. Every agent with a detail page shows the same parts: its purpose, its actions, what starts it, the files it reads and its past runs. This section explains each part, starting with the marketplace where agents live."],[3377,"The agent builder overview walks through the rest of the setup."],[3277,"The agent builder shows what each agent is made of. Its tools and run modes decide which actions run on their own and which wait for approval. Triggers start an agent when something happens, knowledge files give it your material, instructions spell out its process, and run history records what it did."],[320,"The Agent Feed"],[2227,"The agent has five tools: **Analyze Recordings**, **Detect Moments**, **Generate Summary**, **Add to Clip Library** and **Send to Slack**. All five are set to **Auto Run** by default, so they act without asking."],[2990,"The agent marketplace on oliv.ai groups agents by the roles they serve, and lets you pick a pre-built agent or create your own."],[4798,"The agent marketplace on oliv.ai lists pre-built agents for specific jobs, and you can create your own. In the web app, **Agents** opens the same kind of marketplace. Filter by **All**, **Our Agents**, **Sales**, **Revenue Operations** or **Customer Success**. **View Agent** opens an agent your organisation already runs, and **Clone Agent** copies a pre-built one to start from."],[4333,"The agent marketplace: the agents that agent actions run on."],[1631,"The agent reads the call, finds what changed, and proposes updates. That keeps stage, close date and next steps current without chasing anyone."],[2199,"The agent's Overview shows what fills your clip library: it runs when a meeting is completed and looks for the three moment types listed at the bottom."],[4742,"The agents"],[4775,"The agents don't wait for a prompt. They run in the background, start work from signals and schedules, and collaborate with your team and with each other."],[2947,"The agents you talk to"],[174,"The AI adoption ladder for revenue teams: from one-off prompts to agents."],[4551,"The AI answering your question uses those precomputed answers instead of rebuilding context from raw history. Ask which deals are likely to close this quarter, and the answer comes from checking each deal's pain point, compelling event, timeline and MEDDPICC signals. That's the kind of question you'd bring to a pipeline review or to meeting prep."],[2426,"The AI summary and next steps are ready as soon as the call ends. The Slack meeting summary is sent immediately after the meeting ends."],[4121,"The app also matches the Zoom meeting you're in to its calendar event, so the call lands on the right meeting."],[4747,"The apps"],[4808,"The apps are where your team reviews conversations, opportunities and accounts. Every role gets the apps it needs. The apps overview covers how they share one record with the agents."],[2902,"The apps at a glance"],[1914,"The autonomy dropdown under the message box opens on **Ask before acting**, so Olivia aligns on her approach before she takes any action. That setting is one of the points covered in How the Olivia agent compares, below."],[358,"The basic-with-action table adds one column. Here Action shows where each run sent its lead, so the routing is readable without opening a record."],[3674,"The blank fields and the duplicates come from the same place. Fields stay empty because nobody types them after a call, and duplicates appear because people create records by hand. A tool that needs a clean org before it starts will be waiting for a long time."],[1391,"The board is where Forecaster's work rolls up: each manager and rep, their attainment and coverage, and the commit and most likely numbers against the roll-up."],[1595,"The brief arrives 30 minutes before the call. It's built to fit one screen and take about 60 seconds to read."],[4902,"The brief is built to fit on one screen and take about 60 seconds to read, as described under Before a meeting. How this setup lines up against other deal tools is covered in How Oliv AI compares for account executives, below."],[4314,"The calculator states its assumptions. Its figures are comparison assumptions, not vendor quotes, because coverage and contracts differ. Platform fees are excluded. For the thinking behind replacing per-seat tools with agents, see build versus buy for revenue AI."],[3823,"The call already holds the signal. It was said out loud, by a named person, at a known minute in the recording. What's missing is something that hears it and posts it, without waiting for the CSM to write it up."],[2514,"The call itself was recorded. What's missing is a fast way back to it at the moment the AE needs it. A recording only helps if it surfaces before the meeting, not after it."],[4466,"The cases it's built for"],[551,"The catalog is the same whichever agent you opened it from."],[2222,"The Clip button on a meeting's Recording tab is the manual way into the library, for a moment you found yourself before the agent's next run."],[2200,"The clip library gives your team real examples of what good sounds like, taken from your own calls. New reps hear how a pricing objection was handled. Managers coach from a moment instead of a memory."],[2185,"The clip library is where the best moments from your calls live once they've been cut out of the full recording. Libraries are reached through the **Library** tab of the Enable app, which carries the clip library alongside the people tabs. Any clip can also be opened on its own, through a shared view built for people outside your workspace."],[3055,"The coaching side of Enable: clips from real calls grouped into libraries you can keep private or share across the team."],[1429,"The commit is never a bare number: Forecaster explains how it rolled up the reps and where it cut risk, and the snapshot cards show what moved."],[525,"The common answer is a single switch for the whole agent. Teams often keep it read-only for weeks and paste its suggestions into the CRM by hand. That probably feels safe. It also means the agent saves little time, and the pile of suggestions grows with every rep added."],[813,"The common fallback is sitting in on a few calls per hire and scoring them against a rubric by hand. With five people, that might mean a couple of calls each per week. The picture stays thin, and feedback can land after the habit has already formed."],[3967,"The common fallback is to keep notes by hand on those calls, or to record them and promise to review the recording later. Both tend to slip when the week fills up. The account's history then lives in scattered notes, and the next meeting starts with recalling what was agreed."],[962,"The common fix is an hour or two of copying notes into fields, deal by deal. Some fields get rushed and others get skipped. The record ends up showing whatever she had time to type, which may not match what buyers actually said."],[2812,"The Companies tab is where account ranking shows up in Engage. Each account carries its fit score, how engaged it is and the signal that put it in the play, so reps see which account 