recast or Retain app."],[3214,"The proof of value adds one more piece: a tailored version of Oliv AI is live within 1 day of kickoff. How other platforms handle setup and tailoring is covered in How deployment compares, below."],[2219,"The public walkthrough shows the same flow in four steps: **Configure**, **Output**, **Preview results** and **Finish**. Once saved, the moment runs every time a meeting is completed, with no manual trigger. **Run Agent** starts a run by hand if you want one."],[5310,"The qualification status in each brief follows the framework your team runs, whether that's MEDDPICC, MEDDIC, SPICED, BANT or DRIVE, set as **Active** in **Settings › Qualification Framework**. See how Oliv AI scores calls against your sales methodology."],[1267,"The raw signals already exist as the team works. Meetings get held, next steps get set, close dates slip and fields stay blank. What's missing is something that reads those signals every day and rolls them up on a schedule."],[1761,"The real process lives in training decks and in managers' memories. A new rep needs three to six months to learn it. A new methodology or ICP has to be pushed into six tools by hand, and until that happens every rep runs a different version."],[4350,"The reason behind each update"],[5495,"The recap, the commitments, the owners and the next steps, with specific dates where possible."],[4433,"The record and follow up workflow shows the post-call review in practice, and Slack notifications tell reps when updates are waiting."],[5442,"The Recording tab is where you cut and share the moment that matters. Chapters and the speaker timeline help you find it without replaying the whole call."],[2374,"The Recording tab is where you find a moment by chapter or transcript, then clip it, comment on it or share the meeting, without scrubbing through the whole call."],[3984,"The Recording tab of a captured call. Chapters and the transcript help you find a moment fast, and Clip or Share sends it on without rewatching the whole meeting."],[5137,"The records drift because updating them is a separate job from selling. Every call already holds the facts the CRM needs. If those facts flow into the fields as calls happen, the end-of-month scramble shrinks to a check."],[2673,"The remove link in the Zoom chat."],[2672,"The remove link in your meeting preparation summary, by email or Slack."],[5302,"The research brief lands 30 minutes before the meeting, and the full detail view stays in the platform for anyone who wants more. How other platforms prepare reps for a first meeting is covered in How meeting preparation compares, below."],[5576,"The rest of the report sorts the rep's deals into **Closed Won**, **Closed Lost**, **Closing Soon**, **Quick Wins**, **On Track Deals**, **Deals at Risk**, **Existing Customers** and **Needs Follow Up**. Each deal has a summary and a next step."],[3036,"The Retain app covers account management. It comes with the Grow plan."],[803,"The review opens with **Goals for** the month: three goals, the first marked **Recommended**, each with a **Why?** that gives the evidence. Below sit last month's goal with its progress and metric cards against targets, then **Coaching Tasks** with due dates. **Performance Overview** scores the four dimensions, each with a **Strength**, a **Gap** and the change in points. **Drill Down Reports** compare last month with this month against a top performer."],[1151,"The RevOps admin prepares the field list, checks early updates and confirms the switch to auto mode. The weekly governance summary shows each rep's acceptance rate. More on Oliv AI for revenue operations."],[4996,"The RevOps feed brings these together: Oliver's review with changes ready to approve, CRM Manager's count of updates applied and held for review, and Forecaster's category shifts."],[1775,"The RevOps implementation guide walks through the first weeks in more detail."],[3568,"The RevOps integration blueprint: connecting calls, email and Slack to the CRM."],[1141,"The RevOps lead owns this decision, because the reports they build depend on those fields. It matters most during onboarding, and again whenever the team adds a field for a new process or methodology. Sales leaders feel it later, when the reports they read every week finally show complete data."],[4687,"The right rail holds **Ask**, **Comments** and **History**. **Comments** opens a dated team thread beside whichever tab you have open. Type a note or dictate it with the microphone, then send."],[526,"The risk isn't spread evenly across the agent. It sits in the handful of actions that write to shared records or reach customers. Reading a record can't break anything, so it makes sense to hold back only the actions that can."],[695,"The run history on the agent's **Runs** tab records each run's type: **Auto-triggered** or **Auto Trigger** for runs that started on their own, **Manually run** or **Manual** for the rest. That's where you see a trigger at work, as the run history guide in this documentation explains."],[123,"The run mode decides whether that action needs a person's approval, as the guide to agent tools and run modes explains. Every chip opens the **Tools** tab, wherever it sits in a step."],[503,"The run mode is the second half. It sits on each tool card as a dropdown, and it applies to that one tool on that one agent. Changing it on one tool changes nothing else."],[4397,"The run mode sits on each tool card, so you can see at a glance which actions an agent will take without asking."],[381,"The Runs section of the Overview is the way in. See run history opens the Runs tab, and the two cards show at a glance how recently and how often the agent ran."],[344,"The Runs tab is the list layer of run history. Each row is one run, with when it ran, how it started and how it ended."],[1100,"The sales methodology overview explains how framework fields fill from calls."],[3665,"The Salesforce integration is for teams whose records lag behind what customers said. It writes what happened in each call into the records your reports already read."],[3657,"The Salesforce integration lets Oliv AI pull information from your org and populate auto-generated insights back into it, so reps don't update records by hand. Oliv AI works with your existing CRM rather than replacing it. It brings the context together and keeps Salesforce updated, so your existing reports and dashboards run on current data."],[2532,"The same messages go to your email by default. To stop them, turn them off on your **Profile Settings** page. Setup steps are on the Oliv AI integrations page."],[4212,"The same product, seen by a customer success manager: the feed shows the agents that work for that team and role, which is a useful check when you decide who needs a Grow seat."],[2358,"The same summary and full call transcript also land on the opportunity record in the CRM. The CSM can find them there without opening Oliv AI. That CRM record is one of the points covered in How meeting details compares, below."],[826,"The scorecard library covers deals, customer accounts, meetings and phone calls, and the evaluation counts show which ones Coach is actually applying."],[870,"The scorecard library includes phone call scorecards written for SDR work, such as the Cold-Call Scorecard and the Event Follow-Up Call scorecard. BDR leads use the same cadence as sales managers."],[387,"The search box matches different columns on different agents. On most tables it matches the time, type and status, plus the action where there is one. On the contact-oriented table it matches only the contact, company and status. Search there by name, not by date."],[244,"The second section, **What should your agent know?**, holds the upload area."],[3970,"The setup is short enough to follow in one sitting: five steps, all listed under Set up the Webex integration. What the app offers next to other Webex options is covered in How the Webex integration compares, below."],[2277,"The seven tabs"],[4540,"The signal graph stores the answers to common revenue questions before anyone asks them. Oliv AI runs 100+ specialized small language models, each fine-tuned to answer one revenue question. They read every meeting, email and note end to end, with no chunking. The answers are precomputed per deal, per account and per contact, and updated as new information arrives."],[1337,"The signals it raises"],[2839,"The Signals tab is the library a play picks from at step 4. Each signal carries a category and shows whether it comes from first-party or third-party data."],[3808,"The Slack integration connects Oliv AI to your Slack workspace. Oliv uses a collaboration tool to share meeting reminders and meeting summaries, so the team can see what's happening and move deals forward. Slack is one of three collaboration tools listed, next to Microsoft Teams and Google Chat."],[3814,"The Slack integration is for teams that already live in Slack. It puts the meeting prep, the call summary and the account alert where people read their messages. Nobody has to open another tool to find out what happened."],[1497,"The specialist agents"],[4799,"The specialist agents in one place: the ones your team already runs, the templates you can clone, and Oliver on hand to answer questions about any of them."],[666,"The stage change is already the signal. The CRM records the moment a deal moves, so the work can start from that moment rather than from somebody's calendar. What the team needs is a way to tie the agent to the event itself."],[3194,"The starting scorecards for enablement to review: one library across deals, customers, meetings and phone calls, with a usage count beside each."],[362,"the step a sequence reached, such as **Completed Step 2**, then **Escalated to CSM** or **Sequence Completed**."],[113,"The steps are on the tab for anyone to read. Each one has a title and a description, and the tools it uses appear as chips inside the text. How other platforms describe what an agent does is covered in How agent instructions compare, below."],[1854,"The suggestion cards"],[2122,"The summary and next steps are ready as soon as the call ends. A follow-up draft is ready within minutes."],[1252,"The Sunset Summary closes the day by status, from deals won and lost to deals that need follow-up. Its meetings line also confirms how many of the day's calls were recorded."],[1251,"The Sunset Summary's meetings line also says whether the notetaker recorded the day's meetings. Its sections then walk through **Closed Won**, **Closed Lost**, **Closing Soon**, **Quick Wins**, **Deals at Risk**, **Existing Customers** and **Needs Follow up**."],[1099,"The supporting fields count tells you how many CRM fields each criterion needs. Add those fields to your list so framework scores have somewhere to land."],[3481,"The sync covers pipeline, fields, owners, close dates, stages and forecast categories. Writing back to HubSpot is the job of Oliv AI's CRM Manager agent."],[533,"The tab has two columns. A sidebar down the left holds **Add Tool** above the list of the agent's tools. The main pane on the right shows the same tools as cards, in the same order. Press a tool's name in the sidebar and its card scrolls into view."],[379,"The tab is headed **Run history**. Beneath the heading is a search box with the placeholder \"Search previous runs\", then the table of runs in a bordered box."],[101,"The tab opens with a header card carrying the agent's picture, its name and its one-line description. The **Overview** tab carries a separate, shorter **Goal** statement."],[4292,"The team and role filters show which agents each group relies on, a quick way to sanity-check the seat role you give each person."],[4688,"The team's discussion stays on the deal, next to the fields and templates it is about, instead of in a separate chat that never reaches the record."],[4461,"The three graphs underneath Oliv AI's agents, what each one holds, how it stays current, and which agents read it before they act."],[4189,"The three parts of a bill"],[3825,"The timing of each message is fixed and stated. The prep summary comes 10 minutes before a meeting. The list of tomorrow's meetings comes at 4 PM local time, as set out in Meeting messages in Slack. How those messages line up with other tools is covered in How the Slack integration compares, below."],[5498,"The title, date, time, participants and account, plus key takeaways, next steps, talk time and a scorecard. It goes to Slack and email."],[509,"The tool catalog groups actions by the system they come from. These are the same apps Oliv AI works alongside, from your CRM to your inbox."],[4398,"The tools and run modes guide shows where the control sits. The agent builder overview explains how tools fit alongside an agent's instructions and the triggers that start it."],[3575,"The tools Oliv AI connects to, from your CRM and calendar to Zoom and Slack, what each connection is for, and which ones have their own setup guide."],[494,"The Tools tab puts every action an agent may take in one list. Each card carries its own run-mode dropdown, and here all five tools sit on the default, Auto Run."],[3202,"The tracks meet in the handbook. Oliv AI's Oliver agent keeps the rules, and Oliv AI's Olivia agent runs inside them for every rep. Behind both, Oliv AI tailors its signal models to your company. It researches what you sell, takes in your enablement materials and writes tailored prompts, so each model surfaces only the signals that matter to your motion. A tailored version is live within 1 day of the proof of value kickoff. For the wider architecture, read the RevOps guide to agentic AI."],[635,"The Triggers tab answers what starts an agent. Each card names the event in one line and explains when the agent fires; schedules sit in their own section below."],[1494,"The two agents you talk to"],[4743,"The two agents you talk to"],[1509,"The two agents you talk to share one picker. Choose Oliv AI's Olivia agent for deals and meetings, or Oliv AI's Oliver agent for the process rules."],[4037,"The two Zoom apps"],[4402,"The Type column shows which runs a trigger started on its own, so you know which records to check first."],[284,"The upload area lists .csv, json, .pdf, xlsx, xls, txt, md, docx and .pptx. That covers documents, slide decks, spreadsheets and data files."],[237,"The upload area prints its formats: \"Supported formats: .csv, json,.pdf, xlsx, xls, txt, md, docx, .pptx.\"\nThe line beneath it reads \"Max 5 files per upload\". How other platforms handle the files an agent works from is covered in How agent knowledge files compare, below."],[285,"The upload area states \"Max 5 files per upload\"."],[2062,"The usual answer is a recorder for video calls and nothing else. Phone calls stay in the dialer, emails in each rep's inbox and Slack threads in their channels. Someone pieces the story together by hand, which can take an afternoon and still misses the conversations nobody recorded."],[2800,"The usual answer is a sequence tool, a dialer and a spreadsheet. Reps spend the first part of the morning researching, and the list they work from may have been sorted days ago. A good part of the call block can go to preparation rather than conversation."],[2650,"The usual answer is to switch the recorder off and take notes by hand, or to ask the customer for an exception. Either way, something tends to get lost. It's either the record of the call or a little goodwill. Hand-written notes also miss the exact words a customer used about budget or scope."],[4648,"The usual compromise is a point tool with its own database and a nightly sync. Reps then check two screens, and someone in RevOps reconciles them when the numbers disagree. That probably costs a few hours a week, and trust in the pipeline report drops each time the two drift apart."],[5460,"The usual fix is a quick email typed from memory, or a note to write it properly that evening. A rushed recap tends to miss who owns what. An evening recap arrives after the buyer has moved on to other things. Meanwhile the CRM still shows last week's stage and close date."],[665,"The usual fix is a saved CRM report and a reminder. Someone checks the report on Monday and nudges each deal owner in chat. That probably works for a while. But a deal that hits Proposal on a Tuesday can wait six days, and the check quietly stops when its owner goes on leave."],[3350,"The usual fix is to scroll and search before each call, then copy the key points into the CRM by hand. On a busy week that can eat an hour, and it may not happen at all. When it doesn't, the deal record may miss what the buyer actually wrote."],[4102,"The usual habit is to have each SDR press record, or invite a recorder, at the start of every call. That tends to break on busy days. A call hosted by the prospect may never get recorded, and a call whose host is missing can go unrecorded too. The handoff to the account executive then runs on a few typed notes."],[372,"The usual hunt starts in the CRM's field history, which may only show an integration user. From there it's a round of questions to admins and a look through sync settings. That can easily take an hour, and the answer is often a best guess."],[1266,"The usual method is a CRM export, a message to each rep asking for an update, and a skim of recent call notes. It can easily take an evening. Even then, the picture is only as current as the last field each rep remembered to fill in."],[3673,"The usual move is a clean-up project before any new tool goes live. Someone exports accounts, merges duplicates by hand and writes validation rules that force reps to fill fields. That probably takes weeks, and the rules tend to need rework each time a field or a sales stage changes."],[5299,"The usual prep is a quick tour of LinkedIn, the company's website and a news search, plus any notes in the CRM. Reps who do this well tend to spend 15 to 20 minutes per contact. On a full day that time often isn't there, so the research gets skimmed or skipped."],[2355,"The usual route is a short recap message from the AE and a skim of the CRM notes. Some CSMs block an hour to watch the full recording. Each path likely costs time, and a recap written from memory can drop the one integration the champion called a must-have."],[2208,"The usual route is to ask managers for good calls, then scrub the recordings and trim clips by hand. That can take days, and the folder tends to stop growing once the cohort ramps. Next quarter, the examples are already a few months old."],[3211,"The usual route is to clean the CRM, write the playbook down properly and then train everyone at once. Each of those steps can take weeks on its own. By the time they're done, the quarter has moved on and the team has lost interest."],[5136,"The usual routine is an export to a spreadsheet and a round of messages to reps. Duplicates get merged by hand and blank fields get chased one owner at a time. It tends to eat most of a week, and the clean state often lasts only until the next deck."],[4899,"The usual routine is to skim old notes in the few minutes between calls. Each follow-up then gets written from memory at the end of the day, and CRM fields wait until Friday. By then some details have probably faded, so the record shows less than the calls did. The next meeting on each deal may start from a thinner picture as a result."],[1410,"The usual run-up is a round of rep updates, manager check-ins and a rollup sheet that comes together late on Thursday. Across a team, that routine can swallow a dozen hours in a week. Even then, the number tends to lean on how hopeful each rep felt."],[3822,"The usual way is a habit. After a call, the CSM types a short update into the channel, if there's time. Updates can get shorter as the week fills up, and some are skipped. The account executive may learn about an expansion hint weeks later, from a renewal note."],[3947,"The Webex integration is an app called **Oliv.ai** in the Webex App Hub. The App Hub is Webex's own place to find and install apps. Once the app is installed and your Oliv account is connected, Oliv starts joining your Webex calls and summarizing them. Each captured call then appears under **Activities** › **Meetings**, next to calls from every other platform."],[3959,"The Webex integration is for revenue teams whose meetings happen on Webex, often because a customer or their own IT team has standardized on it. It puts those calls in front of Oliv AI's agents, the same way calls on other platforms are."],[900,"The weekly cadence"],[1441,"The Weekly Forecast puts the team's call and Forecaster's own number side by side, then lists the deals behind the gap, ready for the Monday review."],[5007,"The weekly review"],[5029,"The weekly review starts from the Pipeline Review, which groups every deal by status so a manager can jump straight to the deals at risk."],[1197,"The weekly rhythm"],[5268,"The whole prep fits in one answer, from the deal cards down to a brief from Oliv AI's Researcher agent on the attendee you haven't met. The figures and names in it are sample data."],[4777,"The whole product starts here: two agents you talk to in one chat, with every app, record and setting one click away in the sidebar."],[4382,"The workflow for keeping the CRM updated walks through that review step by step, and the CRM Manager agent page covers the agent itself."],[4086,"The Zoom integration connects your Zoom account to Oliv AI. It is how Oliv AI's Converse app captures Zoom calls: it joins Zoom, Meet and Teams automatically and labels every speaker. Once a call ends, the summary and next steps are ready, and a follow-up draft follows within minutes. Every recorded Zoom call then sits under **Activities** › **Meetings**."],[4094,"The Zoom integration is for teams that run their customer calls on Zoom. It makes sure each call is recorded and handed to Oliv AI's agents. The agents then prepare the next call and handle the follow-up."],[5092,"Then check your stages in **Settings › Revenue Process**. **Import Pipeline from CRM** pulls in the stages from the connected CRM, with a tab for each sales motion. Each deal's header also carries **Sync to Salesforce** to push the deal to the CRM."],[3159,"Then connect calendars, your call recorder and Slack or Teams. Calendars tell Oliv AI which meetings are coming. The recorder is the main source of live context, whether that's Oliv AI's own notetaker or a tool you already use. Email is optional but adds context between meetings. Most teams are connected within a week, and the integrations overview shows how each connection fits."],[529,"There are three ways in:"],[376,"There are two ways in:"],[4834,"There's no rip and replace on day one. You connect only what you need today and migrate when you're ready."],[2259,"There's no set length. Each clip is cut to be concise and complete, so it holds the whole moment and little else."],[5253,"There's nothing to request. Oliv AI's Meeting Assistant agent sends the brief automatically before every meeting linked to an active opportunity, 30 minutes ahead of the start time. Oliv AI's Olivia agent works through Slack, Teams or the Oliv AI product, so prep shows up where you already work."],[5094,"There's nothing to schedule. From the first meeting, call or email, Oliv AI's CRM Manager agent is already working. It's the idea behind autonomous CRM hygiene: no rules to maintain. After each activity it:"],[952,"These are the standard fields CRM Manager keeps current, such as close date, amount, stage and next step, grouped the way a rep reads a deal."],[410,"These are typical patterns, shown to illustrate how run history is used."],[3219,"These are typical patterns, shown to illustrate how teams roll Oliv AI out."],[2230,"These are typical patterns, shown to illustrate how teams use clips."],[5148,"These are typical patterns, shown to illustrate how teams use CRM Manager."],[5469,